("olav-tryggvason-and-sigrid-the-haughty",6,0,"According to a saga, King Olav Tryggvason asks the proud Swedish queen Sigrid to marry him and become Christian, she refuses, and he strikes her with his glove. Mårten Eskil Winge painted this tense meeting in 1868."),
("ragnar-lodbrok-and-kraka",6,0,"The famous Viking hero Ragnar Lodbrok sits beside his clever wife Kráka in this scene from the Norse sagas. The Swedish painter August Malmström made it around 1880, when Scandinavians were falling in love with their Viking past."),
("the-death-of-harold-bayeux-tapestry",6,0,"Harold, the last Anglo-Saxon king of England, dies at the Battle of Hastings in 1066. The Bayeux Tapestry, an embroidered strip about 70 meters long, tells the story of the Norman conquest in stitches, and tradition says Harold was killed by an arrow in the eye."),
("norman-knights-and-archers-at-hastings-bayeux-tapestry",6,0,"Norman knights on horses and archers charge in this section of the Bayeux Tapestry, the embroidered story of the conquest of England in 1066. It was made only a few years later, so the artists were close to the events."),
("harold-is-crowned-king-bayeux-tapestry",6,0,"Harold takes the throne of England in January 1066 after King Edward dies, in a scene from the Bayeux Tapestry. William of Normandy said the crown was promised to him, which led to the invasion."),
("harold-swears-an-oath-to-william-bayeux-tapestry",6,0,"Harold swears an oath of loyalty to William of Normandy with his hands on holy relics, in a scene from the Bayeux Tapestry. The Normans later said that Harold broke this promise when he became king."),
("king-edward-the-confessor-bayeux-tapestry",6,0,"The Bayeux Tapestry begins with the old, childless King Edward the Confessor of England on his throne, whose death would lead to the fight for the crown in 1066."),
("horses-in-the-battle-of-hastings-bayeux-tapestry",6,0,"Horses tumble and soldiers fight at the Battle of Hastings in the Bayeux Tapestry. The embroidery is a strip of linen stitched with colored wool in the 1070s, and it is one of the world's most famous works of medieval art."),
("william-gives-harold-arms-bayeux-tapestry",6,0,"William of Normandy gives Harold weapons in this scene from the Bayeux Tapestry. Medieval viewers could read the Latin words stitched above the figures, which act like captions."),
("bishop-odo-william-and-robert-bayeux-tapestry",6,0,"Duke William of Normandy sits with his half-brothers, Bishop Odo and Robert, planning the invasion of England. Odo is thought to have ordered the Bayeux Tapestry to be made."),
("plate-from-the-bayeux-tapestry-stothard",6,0,"In the early 1800s the English antiquarian Charles Alfred Stothard made careful copies of the Bayeux Tapestry for the Society of Antiquaries. His engraved plates, like this one with the Norman fleet, made the tapestry famous in Britain."),
("lady-godiva",6,0,"Edmund Blair Leighton shows Lady Godiva before her famous ride through Coventry, which in the legend she made to persuade her husband to lower the town's taxes. She lived in the years before the Norman Conquest."),
("godiva-preparing-to-ride-through-coventry",6,0,"The legend says Lady Godiva of Coventry agreed to ride through the town to persuade her husband to cut the taxes, while the people promised to stay inside. George Jones painted her preparing to ride in 1833."),
("the-children-s-crusade",6,0,"In 1212 thousands of European children, many in poor families, set off on a march to the Holy Land, and most never got there. Gustave Doré drew this scene for a famous illustrated history of the Crusades in 1877."),
("bohemond-alone-mounts-the-rampart-of-antioch",6,0,"The Norman leader Bohemond climbs the walls of Antioch in 1098 in one of the crusaders' most daring moments. Gustave Doré drew it in dark, dramatic black and white."),
("dandolo-preaching-the-crusade",6,0,"The aged and blind Doge of Venice, Enrico Dandolo, speaks in front of the cathedral to stir up the Fourth Crusade. Gustave Doré illustrated it in 1877."),
("baldwin-of-boulogne-entering-edessa-in-february-1098",6,0,"Baldwin of Boulogne, a leader of the First Crusade, is welcomed into the city of Edessa in 1098, where he became its ruler and founded the first Crusader state. Joseph-Nicolas Robert-Fleury painted it in 1840."),
("richard-the-lionheart-at-the-battle-of-acre",6,0,"Richard the Lionheart of England fights in the siege of Acre during the Third Crusade, in 1191. Philip James de Loutherbourg painted this wild battle around 1807."),
("the-conquest-of-constantinople-by-the-crusaders-in-1204",6,0,"In 1204 crusaders on the Fourth Crusade attacked the Christian city of Constantinople from their ships instead of going to the Holy Land. This French manuscript painting was made around 1470 and shows them swarming the walls."),
("the-council-of-clermont",6,0,"In 1095 Pope Urban II gives a famous speech at Clermont that started the First Crusade, and the crowd shouts 'God wills it!' This illustration comes from a French manuscript made around 1474."),
("saladin-and-guy-of-lusignan",6,0,"Saladin, the great Muslim leader who recaptured Jerusalem in 1187, meets Guy of Lusignan, the king of Jerusalem he had defeated at Hattin. The English monk Matthew Paris drew this lively sketch around 1250."),
("the-capture-of-constantinople",6,0,"Jacopo Tintoretto paints the Venetians and crusaders capturing Constantinople in 1204 as a dark swirl of fighting. He made it in the 1580s for the Doge's Palace in Venice, which took pride in the event."),
("louis-ix-called-saint-louis",1,0,"King Louis IX of France, called Saint Louis, rides on a rearing horse. He led two crusades, was captured on the first, and died on the second in 1270. Émile Signol painted him in 1844 for the French royal museum at Versailles."),
("emperor-frederick-ii-meets-sultan-al-kamil",6,0,"In 1229 Emperor Frederick II made a treaty with Sultan al-Kamil of Egypt, which gave Jerusalem back to the Christians without a battle. This picture comes from the Nuova Cronica of Giovanni Villani, a 14th-century chronicle of Florence."),
